  "answer": "Yes, in many instances—but not simply by making the goods list shorter.\n\n**Act:** Section 43 requires rejection where a connotation of the mark or a sign within it makes use for the particular goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion. The statutory test therefore relates to the goods or services claimed. [TMA1995/s43]\n\n**Manual practice — narrowing the list:** A section 43 objection can often be overcome by restricting the specification so it covers only goods for which the connotation is not deceptive or confusing. The important point is what remains in the specification, not merely that some goods have been deleted. [TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2]\n\n**Manual practice — geographical origin:** For an explicit geographic claim, the Manual identifies a risk of deception if the claim proves false and is not reflected in the specification or an endorsement. A suitably specific endorsement, amendment or limitation can mitigate that risk. [TMM/Part29/9/9/2]\n\n**Manual practice — connection with a person or organisation:** Where a name or representation suggests such a connection, evidence that the connection actually exists would usually be sufficient to overcome the objection. Narrowing is therefore not the only possible response. These passages do not establish that narrowing will cure every false-connection concern. [TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2]"
